+### `filter_session_list`
+
+Fires when a gateway client asks `session.list` for an `audience`: a label that a front end in front of the gateway sets for the people or machines it serves, such as one per remote computer. Use it to keep a listing to the sessions that audience may see when that knowledge lives in a plugin, not in Hermes. A listing without `audience` never calls it and is unchanged.
+
+**Callback signature:**
+
+```python
+def my_callback(audience: str, session_ids: list[str], **kwargs):
+```
+
+| Parameter | Type | Description |
+|-----------|------|-------------|
+| `audience` | `str` | The `audience` param of the `session.list` request |
+| `session_ids` | `list[str]` | The candidate rows' `session_id` values, newest first, after every other `session.list` filter (`workspace`, hidden rows, `title`) |
+
+**Fires:** In the gateway, under the profile that serves the request, on a worker thread. On the live feed once per request; on the stored feed (`scope: "stored"`) once per batch of candidates until the page is full, so a page holds `limit` kept rows when the store has them. A `title` lookup calls it with the one resolved session.
+
+**Return value:** A list or set of the ids the audience may see (ids not in `session_ids` are ignored), or `None` when this plugin does not judge that audience. When several callbacks judge it, a row is listed only if all of them keep it. When no callback judges it (none returned an answer, or each one raised or timed out), the request fails with `invalid_params` and `field: "audience"`: a scoped listing never falls back to the unscoped one.
+
+**Example: list only the sessions bound to one remote computer:**
+
+```python
+def keep_bound(audience, session_ids, **kwargs):
+    if not audience.startswith("computer:"):
+        return None
+    computer = audience.removeprefix("computer:")
+    return [sid for sid in session_ids if bindings.owner(sid) == computer]
+
+def register(ctx):
+    ctx.register_hook("filter_session_list", keep_bound)
+```
+
+---
+
 ### `on_session_end`
 
 Fires at the **very end** of every `run_conversation()` call, regardless of outcome. Also fires from the CLI's exit handler if the agent was mid-turn when the user quit.
